# Review gates and claim strength

The procedure (who runs, in what order) is the `gate-chain` skill. This page explains why,
and holds the self-audit you do before any reviewer sees the text.

## Why fresh-context reviewers{{#if modules.attempts}} (principle: fresh-context-review){{/if}}

The author of a finding is the worst-placed reader of it. They know what every undefined
symbol means, they remember the evidence the text forgot to cite, and they read the hedge
into a headline that does not carry it. A reviewer started with fresh context has none of
that. It reads what is on the page.

So the brief keeps the history out: no author's name, no failed attempts, no author's
confidence, no earlier verdict. Models switch from right answers to wrong ones after reading
what peers concluded, and a famous name makes an argument more persuasive to mathematicians.
The brief does carry what the reviewer cannot get for itself: the intended reading of the
question, and the exact statement of each result a proof cites, since a citation can name a
real paper for a result it does not contain, or a theorem whose hypotheses fail here.

The reviewers are **orthogonal**: each asks one different question, and a document can pass
one while failing another.

- `claim-auditor`: does each conclusion follow from the evidence the text cites, at the
  scope it states? (Logic, not truth.)
- `proof-referee`: is the proof rigorous and readable, and is its load-bearing step right?
- `red-team`: is the claim true? It hunts the cheapest counterexample.

A reviewer is a strong-tier model, never weaker than the author it reviews, because a gate
that misses things is worse than no gate: it turns a guess into a recorded result.

## Which verdict passes, per reviewer

Every reviewer ends with a verdict word. Only the passing word lets you proceed{{#if modules.commit_gate}} (and
only that word satisfies the commit gate; stamp the others too, as the review history){{/if}}.
Any other word means: apply the fixes the reviewer listed, then dispatch it again on the
revised text, up to the re-review limit: three reviews of one document by one reviewer per
cycle (the `gate-chain` skill). At the limit, record the claim at the strength the last
review supports, with its open issues listed, and propose nothing above it. One narrower
case: an argued claim kept as evidence{{#if modules.conjectures}} (at or below weakly-supported){{/if}} gets one claim-auditor pass;
apply its fixes and record, with its verdict noted in the work folder's README and any point
left open in the file. Re-dispatch only if you changed the claim beyond the auditor's own fixes{{#if modules.commit_gate}}; recording evidence
needs no stamp (the commit gate asks for one only when a status changes){{/if}}.
**Why:** past a few rounds, review buys little: in test runs repeated rounds reached fifteen
reviewer calls on one heuristic without changing its verdict. A claim recorded with its open
issues listed is honest; one reviewed until a reviewer gives up is not.

**Every report from the `claim-auditor`, the `proof-referee` or the `red-team`** lists each
issue as `<location> — <what is wrong> — <why it fails> — <the fix>`, marked **major** or
**minor**: minor only if an expert could fix it without anyone reviewing the fix (the
`red-team` lists issues this way when its verdict is not `SURVIVED`).{{#if modules.readers}} The
reader reviewers list issues in their own report forms, without a severity.{{/if}} A `proof-referee` `CLEAN-MINOR` lists minor points and no major one:
apply them, with no second review; `CLEAN` means nothing is left to fix.{{#if modules.readers}} A `cold-reader`
`SELF-CONTAINED-MINOR` works the same way: every hit is a definition, gloss or forward
reference; apply them, with no second review.{{/if}} No verdict carries a confidence figure: a model's stated
confidence predicts its own failures barely better than chance.

| Reviewer | Passing verdict | Other verdicts (fix, then re-dispatch) |
|---|---|---|
| `claim-auditor` | `SOUND` | `OVER-CLAIMED`, `UNSUPPORTED`, `INCONSISTENT` |
| `proof-referee` | `CLEAN`, `CLEAN-MINOR` | `NEEDS-WORK` |
| `red-team` | `SURVIVED` | `REFUTED`, `NARROWED`, `INCONCLUSIVE` |
{{#if modules.readers}}
| `cold-reader` | `SELF-CONTAINED`, `SELF-CONTAINED-MINOR` | `NEEDS-CONTEXT` |
| `expositor` | `READER-READY` | `NEEDS-WORK` |
| `provenance` | `ATTRIBUTION-CLEAN` | `NEEDS-CITATIONS` |
{{/if}}
{{#if modules.questions}}
| `tie-auditor` | `TIED` (on its `OVERALL:` line) | `WEAK/strength`, `WEAK/tie`, `TOPIC-MATCH` |
{{/if}}
{{#if modules.firsthand}}
| `lit-search` | none: it returns a report (`BOTTOM LINE`, `FINDINGS`) | check its citations yourself before using them |
{{/if}}

`NARROWED` from the `red-team` means the claim held but the evidence is thinner than stated:
weaken the claim to what it supports, then ask again.
{{#if modules.questions}}
The `tie-auditor` reviews a task's question tie before the task is dispatched, not a
finding; it is listed so all the verdict words are in one place.
{{/if}}
{{#if modules.conjectures}}

Which of these a conjecture status change needs is the status-authority table in
[conjecture-ledger.md](conjecture-ledger.md#who-sets-a-status-and-what-must-exist-before-the-commit-status-authority).
{{/if}}

## Where staging documents live

A reader-facing write-up is drafted from an internal staging document: the plain record of
what was found, with every hedge. Keep it next to the work it records: in the line of
work's folder `work/<slug>/`{{#if modules.experiments}}, or `experiments/<slug>/analysis.md`{{/if}}, separate from
the write-up. It is the honesty record the write-up is checked against.

## Why this order

Cheapest first, and one at a time on a document, because each stage's fixes change what
the next stage should read. The scans (`bin/hedge-scan`, `bin/define-check`,
`bin/math-hygiene-scan`) cost seconds and remove the mechanical noise a reviewer would
otherwise spend its effort on. The claim-auditor runs before the red-team because scope
errors are cheaper to find than counterexamples, and a red-team pass on an over-scoped text
attacks the wrong statement.

## Why promotion takes two steps and the owner

A status change is drift-prone: the same claim appears in several places, and a partial
edit leaves the text contradicting itself. So the claim-auditor must pass the new wording
first, then the red-team must fail to break it. Only then does the agent propose the change.
The owner makes it, because a promoted claim is what the project builds on next.

## The claim-strength self-audit (before you write a status or a verdict)

Answer these in the entry itself.

1. **Rigorous invariant or proxy?** Does the verdict rest on an exact structural fact (a
   closed form, a proof, an exhaustive check) or on a finite, sampled or narrow proxy? If an
   exact invariant exists, make it the verdict and demote the proxy to a bounded cross-check.
   Do not tune a fragile proxy until it agrees; that inverts which instrument you trust.
2. **Name the scope gap.** State exactly what the evidence covers and what the claim
   asserts. One family, one mechanism or one parameter window is not "in general".
3. **Proposing a new invariant or reframing? Measure the target.** Before recording that a
   cheap quantity determines an expensive one, measure the expensive one directly on at least
   one adversarial case. A proxy that is cheap to falsify must be falsified first, not
   asserted and hedged.
4. **Is the sample capped or chosen for convenience?** A cost cap does not drop cases at
   random: it drops the extreme ones first (the largest, slowest, most degenerate). A rate,
   trend or "always" estimated from the cases that finished is biased toward the cheap ones
   and can reverse when the cap rises. Name what the cap dropped; probe one dropped case if
   it is cheap. Not needed for a genuinely exhaustive or random sample.

When the chain is long or the conclusion feels more confident than the evidence, do not
stop at the self-audit: run the `claim-auditor`.

## A proof of a stronger statement is not a proof of the target

When the deliverable is a proof of a specific statement X, a proof of a stronger Y that
implies X only through a step nobody has proved (a "standard" inclusion, an asserted
bridge) is not progress on X. Lead with X itself; label the stronger statement as an
optional simplification, not the result.

Why: it is easy to declare victory on the surrogate while the step that actually connects
it to X stays open.

Anti-scope: using a more general result to simplify the proof of X is encouraged; this rule
does not apply when Y is itself the agreed goal, or when the bridge from Y to X is proved.

## The load-bearing steps

Most proofs stand on one condition, some on two or three: which map exists, which
decomposition holds, which identity is invoked. A review pass can check every routine step
and still wave one of them through as subtly the wrong object. So the proof-referee
re-derives them first (at most three; in a long chain of equally delicate estimates, the
three riskiest, naming the rest), and so should the author: before building a long
write-up on a proof, re-derive each crux in a few lines yourself. A reviewer's "sound, crux included" does not replace that.
